        cn: '1692 到 1700 年。我 22-30 岁。跟 Gottfried 在 Saxony 跟 Leipzig 工作 8 年。\n\n生 4 个孩子（3 个活到成年，全部进入天文）:\n- Christfried（1694-1740）儿子\n- Christine（1696-1782）女儿\n- Margaretha（1698-1759）女儿\n\nGottfried 跟我用同一台望远镜，同一份数据，同一个 Latin 写的报告。\n\n但 1700 年发生一件大事——\n\n1700 年 7 月 11 日——Brandenburg（勃兰登堡，普鲁士前身）选帝侯 Friedrich III 创办 Berlin Akademie der Wissenschaften（柏林科学院）。\n\n第一任 President 是 Leibniz（你 Lens 1 跟 Lens 2 都见过那个 Leibniz——Newton 微积分对手——也是 Mersenne 通信网的「孙辈」）。\n\nLeibniz 把模型从巴黎搬来: 1660 London Royal Society + 1666 Paris Académie + 1700 Berlin Academy——欧洲三大科学院的德国版。\n\nBerlin Academy 有一个主要 revenue source: 卖 calendar（历法）。每年帝国农民要买 calendar 看播种时节。所以 Academy 第一任 astronomer 的工作是「编 calendar + 观测」——institutional 重要。\n\nGottfried 1700 年 7 月任 Berlin Academy 第一任 astronomer + 月薪 500 thaler（银币——当时柏林一个工匠年薪约 100-150 thaler——所以 500/月 = 中产阶级）。\n\n我没有 official 职位。\n\n我搬进 Academy 观测台旁的公寓，跟 Gottfried + 4 个孩子住——\n\n我每天晚上用 Academy 的望远镜: 观测、记录、校对 Gottfried 的报告。\n\n但 Academy 的 payroll 上没有我名字。\n\nSchiebinger 1989 (p. 86) 一句很 sharp 的话: 「这跟现代「家庭主妇 GDP 不计入」是同一逻辑的 17c 版本。」\n\n你 21 世纪可能听过「无偿家务劳动」这个词。1700 年的 Berlin 已经在用同一种逻辑——只是给天文学家妻子用。',
        en: 'From 1692 to 1700 — I was 22 to 30 — Gottfried and I worked in Saxony and Leipzig for eight years.\n\nFour children (three lived to adulthood, all became astronomers):\n- Christfried (1694-1740), son\n- Christine (1696-1782), daughter\n- Margaretha (1698-1759), daughter\n\nGottfried and I used one telescope, one set of data, one Latin report.\n\nThen 1700 happened.\n\nOn 11 July 1700, Friedrich III, Elector of Brandenburg (predecessor of Prussia), founded the Berlin Akademie der Wissenschaften — the Berlin Academy of Sciences.\n\nIts first president was Leibniz (the same Leibniz from Lens 1 and Lens 2 — Newton\'s rival in the calculus dispute, and a "grandchild" of Mersenne\'s correspondence network).\n\nLeibniz copied the model from Paris: 1660 London Royal Society + 1666 Paris Académie + 1700 Berlin Academy — the German version of Europe\'s three great academies.\n\nBerlin Academy had one main revenue stream: selling calendars. Every year the empire\'s farmers needed a calendar to know planting dates. So the first astronomer\'s job was "edit the calendar and observe" — institutionally central.\n\nGottfried became the Academy\'s first astronomer in July 1700 with a monthly salary of 500 thaler (a Berlin craftsman earned roughly 100 to 150 thaler a year, so 500 a month was solid middle class).\n\nI had no official post.\n\nWe moved into the apartment beside the Academy observatory — Gottfried, the four children, and me.\n\nEvery night I used the Academy telescope — observed, recorded, edited Gottfried\'s reports.\n\nMy name was not on the Academy payroll.\n\nSchiebinger 1989, p. 86, a sharp sentence: "This is the 17th-century version of the same logic that today excludes housework from GDP."\n\nIn the 21st century you may have heard "unpaid household labor." Berlin in 1700 already ran the same logic — just on the wife of an astronomer.',
